{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Philadelphia and Reading Railroad's namesake Main Line, up the Schuylkill River from Philadelphia through Reading to the Pottsville anthracite coal region -- the railroad literally on the Monopoly board. Opened Philadelphia-Reading in 1838, extended to Pottsville by 1842. Renamed the Reading Company after 1924 reorganization; rail operations absorbed into Conrail in 1976, with SEPTA taking over as subsidized commuter operator (branded the 'Pottsville Line'/'Reading Line') until final discontinuance July 26 1981. No passenger rail has served Reading since -- Amtrak currently runs only a Thruway coach bus over this corridor as a placeholder pending a proposed restoration. GEOMETRY METHODOLOGY: the Philadelphia-Reading segment (93.6 km) is real-traced through OpenStreetMap active-rail data (Overpass API, shortest-path graph search), since that stretch remains active SEPTA/Norfolk Southern trackage. The Reading-Pottsville segment through the coal region is hand-placed waypoints through the real historic stations (Leesport, Hamburg, Schuylkill Haven) -- Overpass found no connected path for this stretch even with abandoned/disused ways included, consistent with the more fragmentary state of ex-Reading trackage north of Reading itself.", "source": "Wikipedia (Main Line (Reading Company), Pottsville Line, Reading Company, Reading, Pennsylvania), american-rails.com (Reading Railroad history), goreadingberks.com (End of the Line -- Last Passenger Train to Reading)", "pulled": "20260818", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Philadelphia and Reading Railroad / Reading Company", "line": "Philadelphia and Reading: Philadelphia \u2013 Pottsville", "id": "reading-philadelphia-pottsville", "color": "#2A6F97", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 55, "speed_source": "estimated: mixed commuter/intercity/coal-region service; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1838, "end": 1924, "headway_min": 240, "note": "Philadelphia-Reading opened May 1 1838, extended to Pottsville by 1842, following the Schuylkill River through the anthracite coal region; general/named intercity and coal-traffic service under the Philadelphia and Reading Railroad; frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Main Line (Reading Company), Pottsville Line, Reading Company, Reading, Pennsylvania), american-rails.com (Re\u2026"]}, {"start": 1924, "end": 1976, "headway_min": 180, "note": "Renamed the Reading Company after 1924 reorganization; post-WWI electrification reached the inner Philadelphia commuter segments while steam/diesel continued serving Reading and Pottsville further out; frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Main Line (Reading Company), Pottsville Line, Reading Company, Reading, Pennsylvania), american-rails.com (Re\u2026"]}, {"start": 1976, "end": 1981, "headway_min": 360, "note": "Reading Company rail operations absorbed into Conrail in 1976; SEPTA continued subsidized commuter ser